you may think of glaciers as slow-moving  behemoths grinding their way centimeters  at a time but some glaciers can suddenly  shift gears flowing many metres per hour  before slowing down often for weeks or  months and such surges appear to be on  the rise a glacier is said to be surging  when it flows at least 10 times faster  than its usual pace many surges are too  slow for the naked eye to perceive but  time lapses of glaciers reveal their  movements thousands of glaciers are  perched near human settlements and  dozens of searches over the past few  decades have claimed lives not only do  surges pose a threat to nearby  settlements but they can block rivers  create lakes that later unleash floods  and threaten the flow of meltwater that  towns and farms downstream depend on  until recently researchers believe that  it glaciers physical characteristics and  properties of the local terrain  determined surge potential but now they  believe another factor plays a major  role thermal balance pooling on the  surface during the melt season water can  infiltrate a glacier through crevices  and ultimately reach the base warming  lubricating and finally releasing the  ice  [Music]  neighboring glaciers in western Tibet  experienced back-to-back surges in 2016  killing nine people computer modeling  suggested that increased precipitation  and warming boosted the amount of  meltwater by 50 percent before the  surges with more water to seep through  the cracks and eat the ice away the  glacier tongues finally became unmoored  resulting in the surges to stay out of  trouble glaciers need to keep in balance  researchers have observed that glaciers  more easily maintain thermal balance in  cold dry climates where they can shed  heat to the frigid air and warm humid  environments where they can discharge  heat in steady streams of meltwater  models predict that glaciers that don't  meet these conditions are more prone to  search  [Music]  sure enough surging glacier hot spots  are in temperate latitude mountains such  as Karakoram and at lower altitudes like  Svalbard if thermal balance is an  important trigger for surge then climate  change will have an impact recent  findings suggest that a warming world  may be boosting the number of surges in  some regions in Karakoram from 1990 to  2004 two times the number of surges  occurred than in the previous 14 years  analysis of searches of the Lowell  glacier from 1948 to 2009 showed the  time between surges has shortened from  20 years to 12 years scientists hope  that by continuing to track and model  surges they can figure out why glaciers  move at such wildly different rates if  their findings yield insights into the  dynamics behind search perhaps they can  better anticipate sea-level changes and  ultimately save lives  you
 

